  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 938 PM MDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nDRY VALLEYS. ISOLATED RAINFALL IN THE SAN JUANS:\n\nDaily isolated showers and weak thunderstorms continue \nprimarily across the high terrain of the San Juans through \nSaturday. This is thanks to above normal moisture which has \nsettled in across the region and will persist through the next \nseveral days. However, without much in the way of supportive \nforcing or lift, storm activity should be sparse and on the \nweaker side. The one caveat to this is a shortwave descending \nthrough the Great Basin. By Tuesday, we'll sit in on the eastern\nedge of the shortwave, and while a slight bolstering of upper-\nlevel dynamics has led to an increase in precipitation potential\nTuesday and Wednesday, it only appears to modestly boost storm\ncoverage across the high terrain. Otherwise, this pattern keeps\ntemperatures above normal across the CWA, particularly along \nand north of I-70 where some locations could reach 15 degrees \nabove normal.\n\nPATTERN SHIFTS BEYOND FRIDAY:\n\nWe've been eying a stronger system potentially passing through \nthe region sometime early next week. This is being driven by a \nstrong trough expected to skirt across the PacNW and, \neventually, parts of the Rockies. Models have struggled to this \npoint to pinpoint the finer details of the system, but \nconsistency between models is improving. We're still seeing too \nmuch disagreement to draw any strong conclusions, but clusters \nhave begun to more heavily favor a wetter scenario with \nprecipitation focused around Monday and Tuesday (and perhaps \nbeyond). Jet support looks to be present during this period as\nwell, though its strength is not well agreed upon by models \neither, so that remains a major question mark as well. Keep an \neye on the forecast this week to see how guidance shifts \nregarding this system.",
